Give the window server somewhere to draw.

The display shim got iOS as far as a display that CAWindowServer finds,
builds and adds, and then the trace stopped dead:

    IOMobileFramebuffer: [2] selector 74 ... 72 ... 73

and never a 3, 4, 5 or 6. Those are GetLayerDefaultSurface, SwapBegin,
SwapEnd and SwapWait -- the swap path -- and none of them can be reached
without a surface to swap. There is no IOSurface in open-source xnu and no
kext here to bring one, so IOServiceNameMatching("IOCoreSurfaceRoot") finds
nothing and the whole path above it stays folded up.

This adds the service, the five selectors that matter, and the swap.

Everything about the user client was read out of
/System/Library/Frameworks/IOSurface.framework on the device image, the same
way the display shim's selectors were. Calls inside a dylib extracted from
the shared cache branch straight to the callee's absolute address rather
than through a stub, so the sites were found by resolving __la_symbol_ptr
first -- IOConnectCallMethod is at 0x181124f48 -- and each site names itself
through the framework's own C symbols. The file's own comment carries the
table and the output struct layout; what matters here is that the framework
never imports IOConnectMapMemory, so create_surface has to map the memory
and return the address inside its reply, and the reply's shape was taken
from the parser at 0x1821dc424 and cross-checked against the accessors.

The swap end of it is three cases in the display shim. There is no scanout
to repoint -- the controller was pointed at the console framebuffer by the
bootloader and nothing in this tree moves it -- so a swap is a copy, from
the surface layer 0 names to the pixels already on screen. A full-screen
memcpy per frame on one Cortex-A53 is the price of having no GPU, and it is
replaceable later without anything above noticing.

Selector 3 is left refused on purpose. It hands back a surface the driver
owns and this driver owns none; a zero there would send a caller off with an
id that resolves to nothing, which is the kind of wrong answer that travels.

Boot with iosurftrace=0 for none of the new chatter.

Two things the first boot with this made plain.

The kernel console draws into the very framebuffer a swap copies into, so
every IOLog repaints over the frame the window server just put there. A
real display driver takes the framebuffer away from the console when it
comes up; SwapEnd now does that, on the first copy that actually found its
surface, so a swap naming a surface we do not have still leaves the console
up to carry the diagnostic.

The other is why no swap ever arrived. The trace stopped dead after

    IOMobileFramebuffer: [2] registerNotificationPort type 3 refCon 0
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [2] notifications on for type 3
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [2] selector 72 ... 73

and the reason is in this file: the port and the callback were remembered
and nothing was ever sent to them. The window server adds the run loop
source that port feeds and then waits on it. Reading QuartzCore out of the
device image, what hangs off that wait is the whole of display bring-up --
IOMFBServer::frame_info_callback reaches IOMFBDisplay::initialize_timings,
and vsync itself only ever starts from IOMFBServer::did_set_mode or
set_enabled, which are

    remove_vsync_source -> initialize_timings -> add_vsync_source

so with no first callback there is no timing, no mode, no frame clock, no
composite and no swap. The EAGLServer constructor's own add_vsync_source is
not a second chance: it is gated on IOMFBDisplay::framebuffer_id(), which
reads a field at +0x240 that nothing in QuartzCore ever writes.

So this gives the driver the clock it was pretending to have. A timer on
the user client sends the frame-info notification, and the vsync one once
anything arms it, through setAsyncReference64 and sendAsyncResult64 with
the port, callback and context the framework already handed over. The
shapes are the framework's: FrameInfoNotifyFunc drops anything with fewer
than two arguments and says so, reading args[0] as a 64-bit swap id and
args[1] as a 32-bit count; the vsync callback takes three times. There is
no real vblank to follow, so the rate is made up -- 60 Hz, boot-arg
iomfbtickhz, 0 to stop it.
The first boot with that clock showed the other half of it. backboardd took
the notification and died on the spot, sig 11, pc 0x54014a00 against a
refcon of 0x154014A00 -- it had jumped to the low half of its own context.
setAsyncReference64's four argument form leaves kIOUCAsync64Flag clear, so
sendAsyncResult64 sends the msg32 layout with every reference slot REF32'd,
and a 64-bit reader takes the func out of the wrong place. The five
argument form, given the owning task, sets the flag.

With that fixed the display configures and then nothing happens at all, and
nothing is the hardest thing to read: the kernel prints a line when a
process dies and none when one lives, so a daemon that is up and waiting
looks exactly like one that never started, and those two want opposite
fixes. Boot-arg proclist=<seconds> prints the pid table once, that many
seconds in, and then never again.

